{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"body":"## Checkpoint 2026-09-14 — Wave 3 at the halfway mark, after a weekly-limit interruption\n\n**Wave 3 arms:** superpowers 20/20, mini 20/20, CE 10/20. CE is the sole bottleneck.\n**Wave 3 judging:** 9 runs scored and adjudicated; the 10th (`ts-strata-paidleave-documents-tab`)\nhas its ballot frozen and its panel running now.\n\n### The headline, and it is the reason Wave 3 exists\n\nPooled over the 9 scored runs: CE 149 TP / 2 FP (98.7% precision, 75% materiality),\nsuperpowers 123 / 1 (99.2%, 76%), mini 52 / 3 (94.5%, 73%).\n\n**The CE-to-superpowers true-positive ratio is 1.21x here against 2.42x in Wave 1.** Both arms\nhold their Wave 1 precision and materiality almost exactly; what collapses on unfamiliar\nthird-party codebases is specifically CE's recall advantage — for roughly seven times the token\nspend. Head-to-head is 5 CE / 4 SP. Superpowers now leads on both quality measures.\n\n**Retired hypothesis, recorded so it is not carried forward:** mid-wave I noted that CE seemed to\nwin multi-subsystem epics while superpowers won design-system documents, cleanly 4-3 with no\ncrossovers. That broke at n=8 on oscer member-facing status. It is not a finding.\n\n### Two instrument defects found, measured, and deliberately NOT corrected\n\n1. **mini never received the `## Declared file impact` section on any Wave 1 plan** — present in\n   206 plan documents, 0 mini prompts, because `gen_inputs.py` appends it to `plan.md` only. CE\n   and superpowers reviewed it; mini structurally could not. A caveat on every Wave 1 mini\n   *recall* number, not on its precision. Wave 3 is unaffected (no third-party plan has it).\n2. **`harness/runs.json` is not blind-safe** — it carries rebar's verdict, finding counts and\n   blocking criteria in the same record the arms are told to resolve their checkout from. Found\n   because the superpowers Q3 reviewer flagged it unprompted. A scan of all 70 Wave 1 CE and\n   superpowers artifacts found zero references to any verdict field, so no evidence of\n   contamination, but nothing enforced it. Wave 3 is structurally immune.\n\nCorrecting either would invalidate scored runs to fix something already measured and written\ndown, so both are logged in `harness/FIDELITY_LOG.md` as caveats instead.\n\n### A retraction of my own\n\nI dispatched three CE personas on strata-documents believing the orchestrator's fan-out was\nshort. It was not — the orchestrator ran all seven, and I had misread a live-agent snapshot as a\nroster. The duplicates are quarantined outside the ballot path and excluded from arm totals by an\nexplicit flag rather than deleted, so the waste stays visible. Full retraction in the fidelity log.\n\n### Wave 2 Q3 restarted\n\nThe CE pilot, recomputed on the now-pinned no-P0 predicate, reads 2/3/3 across three cycles —\nflat, not converging. Rather than extend that one expensive arm to the cap, the unrun arms go\nfirst: superpowers cycle 1 is done (verdict `Issues Found`, 4 blockers) at roughly a sixth of one\nCE cycle. Rationale in `wave2/q3/STATUS.md`; next step is mini cycle 1.\n\n### The interruption\n\nA weekly-limit cutoff killed 10 live agents. Nothing was corrupted — agents write their artifact\nonce at the end, so an interrupted agent leaves no file rather than a partial one. Nothing was\nsalvageable either; the strata-docs panel and the thunderbird CE run are re-run whole rather than\nrecovered, and both are back in flight.\n\nCost figures live in the study's own cost ledger and local reports, not here."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789413959143671001,"uuid":"13fffcdf-ddeb-4d1a-8212-77d6ec054d04"}